.SH "BUGS"
\fBexit \-1\fR is used for error exits\. POSIX does not guarantee the behaviour of negative exit codes; most shells interpret \fB\-1\fR as 255, but this is not portable\.
.P
In both non\-running branches, \fBvi\fR is launched before \fBexit \-1\fR is reached\. This means the script always opens an editor regardless of error state, which may be surprising in automated or non\-interactive contexts\.
.P
The glob \fB$RECORD_START_PROGRAM\.????\-??\-??\.out\fR is unquoted and may expand to nothing (or cause an error with \fBnullglob\fR unset) if no matching files exist in the current directory\. \fBvi\fR will then be invoked with a literal unexpanded glob string as its argument\.
.P
If the glob matches more than one file, \fBvi\fR will open all of them as a buffer list, which may or may not be the intended behaviour\.
.P
\fBget_stderr\fR relies on \fB/proc/<pid>/fd/2\fR, which is Linux\-specific and will not work on macOS or other non\-Linux systems\.
.P
The script checks for the existence of \fB/proc/<pid>\fR as a proxy for whether the process is running, which is Linux\-specific\. On systems without \fB/proc\fR, the live\-process branch will never be taken\.
.P
The \fBpid_of_record_run\fR variable is populated by \fBlib\.record\.common\.sh\fR only when the PID file already exists at source time\. If the PID file was created after sourcing the library, \fBpid_of_record_run\fR will be empty and the script will fall through to the not\-running branch even if the process is live\.
